Subject: keep System V shared memory inside proot

ThothDock passes --sysvipc so that PostgreSQL and other System V IPC users
work on Android, whose kernel is built without CONFIG_SYSVIPC (shmget and
friends fail with ENOSYS there). Upstream's extension, in the
per-proot namespace mode ThothDock selects (PROOT_DONT_SHARE_LIBANDROID_SHMEM),
has these problems:

- every proot that uses shared memory forks a "proot --shm-helper" process
  that lives as long as proot: one more process per database container,
  against Android's phantom-process cap;
- the helper allocates from /dev/ashmem, which apps targeting API 29+ may
  not open, so shmget would fail with ENOSPC in the app;
- a SIGKILL of proot's process group leaves the helper's socket file in
  PROOT_TMP_DIR;
- two processes calling shmat at the same moment can receive each other's
  segment: attaches were serialized only from the connect step, so two
  connections could queue on the helper's one socket (4 of 20 runs of
  tests/regression/ipc-probe.c on a desktop host);
- semtimedop returns EINTR instead of EAGAIN when its timeout expires.

In that mode proot now keeps the segments itself, as memfd_create(2)
descriptors (/dev/ashmem or a temporary file only where memfd is missing),
and hands one to the tracee at shmat over a socket that exists only for
that attach and accepts only the tracee's own thread group (SO_PEERCRED).
Attaches are serialized from the socket step. No process is forked, nothing
runs while no IPC call is made, and the memory goes with proot's
descriptors when the container ends. A new segment of size 0 is refused
with EINVAL, as Linux does. libandroid-shmem sharing mode is unchanged.

Upstream: termux/proot @ 7266fb3e8516535682f5a9c8f3a7e70f6506eddb (v5.1.107.92), after patches 0001-0009

